### 1. Task outcome (short version):
- Across large RCTs, adjuvant progestagens did not improve overall survival or relapse-free/disease-free survival.
- One single-institution RCT (n=205) reported improved overall survival with adjuvant progestagens (log-rank P<0.001).
- Safety: a large RCT (n=1148) reported higher non-cancer (intercurrent) mortality in the progestagen arm (P=0.04).

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
Study 1 (Stage I risk-stratified, multicentre RCT; ITT N=856, FU 84 months): MPA 100 mg PO BID x12 months vs 
risk-adapted controls (incl. RT ± MPA). No significant difference in relapse-free survival between MPA and control 
across strata; adjuvant progestagen did not improve cure rate.

Study 5 (Early EC Stage I–II, RCT; N=1148; FU 42–132 months, median 72): Adjuvant progesterone vs observation. Crude OS 
and relapse rates similar. Non-cancer (intercurrent) deaths higher with progestagen (P=0.04). Among those with 
cancer-related death, median survival longer with progestagen (30 vs 22 months; P=0.03). High-risk subgroup (N=461) 
showed a tendency toward fewer cancer deaths and better DFS, but no overall crude survival benefit.

Study 7 (Yorkshire RCT; N=429): Progestogens added to primary treatment (surgery + RT) vs no progestogen. Projected 
5-year OS 76% overall; no statistically significant OS difference between groups, even after adjustment for prognostic 
factors.

Study 10 (Single-institution RCT; N=205; FU 5 years): Adjuvant progestagen vs observation post-hysterectomy. 
Significantly longer survival in the progestagen group (log-rank P<0.001). No effect sizes or CIs reported.

Effect measures: Abstracts do not report HR/RR/OR or 95% CIs; only p-values for select outcomes (intercurrent deaths 
P=0.04; median survival among cancer deaths P=0.03; OS P<0.001 in the single-institution trial). Dosing details provided
only in Study 1 (MPA 100 mg PO BID for 12 months). Other trials did not report agent/dose/schedule in the abstract.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
Limitations: Effect sizes (HR/RR/OR) and 95% CIs are not available from abstracts; full texts are needed for precise 
estimates and adverse event profiles. Histology, age distributions, and detailed stage breakdowns are not consistently 
reported. Heterogeneity: One included trial (Study 7) assessed progestogens as part of primary treatment with surgery 
and radiotherapy rather than purely adjuvant use, which may limit comparability to strictly adjuvant trials. The 
single-institution positive trial contrasts with larger multicentre trials showing no OS/DFS benefit; potential 
differences in patient selection, staging, dosing, or concurrent therapies warrant scrutiny.
